<ArticleLayout title="Employer guide to Trump Account contributions" description="A non-legal implementation brief for HR, payroll, and small-business teams evaluating a Section 128 Trump Account contribution program." sourceIds={['DOL-TR-2026-02','IRS-PROPOSED-EMPLOYER-2026','IRS-FORM-4547-INSTRUCTIONS']} effective="Guidance available through October 8, 2026">
  <div class="callout"><strong>Professional review required:</strong> this page is an educational checklist, not tax, legal, payroll, benefits, or ERISA advice. Proposed regulations remain subject to change.</div>
  <h2>What the benefit can do</h2>
  <p>A qualifying program may make employer contributions to an employee’s Trump Account or a dependent’s Trump Account. Under the current framework, up to $2,500 per employee per year may be excluded from federal gross income when program requirements are satisfied; the amount also counts toward the account’s general $5,000 annual limit.</p>
  <h2>Implementation checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Engage counsel and tax advisers.</strong> Confirm the status and applicability of DOL guidance and Treasury/IRS proposed regulations.</li>
    <li><strong>Draft a separate written plan.</strong> Define eligibility, contribution amounts, plan year, designation steps, notices, reporting, and correction procedures.</li>
    <li><strong>Design certifications.</strong> Collect only what is needed to support dependent, growth-period, and account-status certifications.</li>
    <li><strong>Coordinate payroll.</strong> Map contribution codes, applicable wage-tax treatment, limits across pay periods, and year-end reporting.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify the receiving account.</strong> Establish a process to confirm that the destination is a Trump Account without steering employees to a required trustee.</li>
    <li><strong>Test nondiscrimination.</strong> Review eligibility, benefits, and average-benefit requirements before launch and during operation.</li>
    <li><strong>Prepare employee notices.</strong> Explain eligibility, elections, limits, tax treatment, and the employee’s role in monitoring other contributions.</li>
    <li><strong>Document corrections.</strong> Plan for over-limit amounts, failed certifications, and incorrect tax treatment.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Payroll and limit risks</h2>
  <p>The $2,500 limit applies at the employee level across employer contribution programs. Spouses employed separately may each have their own employee-level limit, while a single employee cannot multiply the limit by the number of dependents. Proposed rules include further allocation examples.</p>
  <h2>What not to promise employees</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not describe the account as guaranteed or government-insured investment performance.</li>
    <li>Do not imply every child receives the $1,000 pilot contribution.</li>
    <li>Do not describe proposed employer regulations as final.</li>
    <li>Do not give individualized tax or investment recommendations in enrollment materials.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Also review the <a href="/contributions/trump-account-contribution-limits/">contribution limit guide</a> and the official DOL and IRS materials before implementation.</p>
